## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern: **Timeout enforcement**

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Your code must shift from *preemptive* abort to *cooperative* cancellation with `CancellationToken`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        // Task.Run replaces manual Thread creation
        var task = Task.Run(work);

        try
        {
            // WaitAsync throws TimeoutException if the task doesn't
            // complete within the timeout (.NET 6+ API)
            return task.WaitAsync(timeout).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (TimeoutException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

> **Important caveat:** The timed-out work keeps running in the background because there's no `Thread.Abort` equivalent. If `work` must actually *stop*, read the upgraded version below.

---

### Upgraded Version (work actually stops)

This requires `work` to accept a `CancellationToken` — the **cooperative** model:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Executes work with a timeout. The work receives a CancellationToken
    /// and must check it periodically for cancellation to take effect.
    /// </summary>
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return computedResult;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Work doesn't check the token** | Cancellation is *cooperative* — if `work` never calls `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`, it runs to completion regardless of the timeout. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` with a timer leaks if not disposed. Always use `using`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Only catch it when `cts.IsCancellationRequested` is true, otherwise you may swallow user-initiated cancellations from an outer scope. |
| **Expecting instant termination** | Cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint. Place `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps. |
| **Blocking I/O ignores the token** | `Thread.Sleep`, `socket.Read`, etc. won't respond. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O overloads that accept a token. |

---

### Removed APIs → Replacements

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---------------|--------|
| `new Thread(…)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(…, token)` |
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` (cooperative) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` and decide |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.WaitAsync(timeout)` |

### Uncooperative Code Escape Hatch

If `work` calls third-party code you **cannot modify** to check a token, move it to a **child process** and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
